{"id": "getty-65f6fd18-042f-4228-8073-41a506d1ce2a", "status": "ok", "meta": "A formal Dutch garden with a promenade, farmland and a shipping scene, painted by Johannes Janson in 1766.", "about": "A promenade at the left of this formal Dutch garden leads to farmland, and a path at the right leads to a shipping scene. The Getty says these were the two main sources of Holland's wealth. In the foreground, flower beds laid out in embroidery patterns are filled not with flowers but with contrasting soil or gravel, framed by small box hedges. Prosperous Dutch citizens of the 1700s hired painters like Johannes Janson to record their gardens. The estate may have been in North Holland.", "museum_record": "https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RDR", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103RDR"], "check": "clean"}
